This means if we follow the edges $i \to A_i \to A_{A_i} \dots$, the values must be non-decreasing.
If we encounter a cycle, say $c_1 \to c_2 \to \dots \to c_k \to c_1$, then $x_{c_1} \le x_{c_2} \le \dots \le x_{c_k} \le x_{c_1}$, so $x_{c_1} = x_{c_2} = \dots = x_{c_k}$.
Let $S$ be the set of vertices. We can decompose $S$ into components. Each component has a unique cycle.
Let's denote the cycle in a component as $C$. All nodes in $C$ must have the same value. Let this value be $v$.
For any node $u$ in the component, there is a path from $u$ to some node in $C$. Let the path be $u \to v_1 \to v_2 \dots \to c \in C$. Then $x_u \le x_{v_1} \le \dots \le x_c = v$.
So $x_u \le v$.
