The grid consists of squares defined by integer coordinates. Specifically, a square can be identified by its center $(i, j)$ where $i, j$ are integers. The vertices of the square centered at $(i, j)$ are $(i \pm 0.5, j \pm 0.5)$.
The condition for a square centered at $(i, j)$ to be completely inside the circle of radius $R$ is that all its vertices must be at a distance $\le R$ from the origin.
The distance of a vertex $(x, y)$ from the origin is $\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}$.
The vertices of the square centered at $(i, j)$ are $(i+0.5, j+0.5)$, $(i+0.5, j-0.5)$, $(i-0.5, j+0.5)$, and $(i-0.5, j-0.5)$.
