The problem asks us to calculate the sum of $f(P)$ over all permutations $P$ of the sequence $(1, 2, \ldots, N)$, modulo 998244353.
$f(P)$ is formed by concatenating the decimal representations of the numbers in the permutation.
Let $A = (A_1, A_2, \ldots, A_N)$ be a permutation of $(1, 2, \ldots, N)$.
$f(A)$ is the integer formed by concatenating $A_1, A_2, \ldots, A_N$.
We need to compute $\sum_{P \in S_N} f(P) \pmod{998244353}$.

Let $L_i$ be the number of digits in the decimal representation of $i$.
For a permutation $P = (P_1, P_2, \ldots, P_N)$, the value $f(P)$ can be written as:
$f(P) = \sum_{i=1}^N P_i \times 10^{S_i}$, where $S_i$ is the number of digits contributed by elements $P_{i+1}, \ldots, P_N$.
Specifically, $S_i = \sum_{j=i+1}^N L_{P_j}$.
So, $f(P) = \sum_{i=1}^N P_i \times 10^{\sum_{j=i+1}^N L_{P_j}}$.

We want to compute $\sum_{P} f(P) = \sum_{P} \sum_{i=1}^N P_i \times 10^{\sum_{j=i+1}^N L_{P_j}}$.
By linearity of expectation (or just swapping sums), this is equal to:
$\sum_{i=1}^N \sum_{P} P_i \times 10^{\sum_{j=i+1}^N L_{P_j}}$.

Let's fix a position $i$ in the permutation (from 1 to $N$). We want to sum $P_i \times 10^{\sum_{j=i+1}^N L_{P_j}}$ over all permutations $P$.
The term $10^{\sum_{j=i+1}^N L_{P_j}}$ depends only on the set of numbers $\{P_{i+1}, \ldots, P_N\}$ and their lengths.
Let $K = N - i$ be the number of elements after position $i$.
The set of elements $\{P_{i+1}, \ldots, P_N\}$ is a subset of $\{1, \ldots, N\}$ of size $K$.
Let this subset be $S_{suffix}$. Then $P_i$ is the remaining element not in $S_{suffix}$ (and not in $\{P_1, \ldots, P_{i-1}\}$).
Actually, it's easier to think about the contribution of each number $x \in \{1, \ldots, N\}$ to the total sum.
For a specific number $x$, suppose it is placed at position $i$ in the permutation.
Then the contribution of $x$ to the sum is $x \times 10^{\sum_{j=i+1}^N L_{P_j}}$.
The exponent depends on the set of numbers placed at positions $i+1, \ldots, N$.
Let $Y$ be the set of numbers placed at positions $i+1, \ldots, N$. Then $|Y| = N - i$.
The contribution is $x \times 10^{\sum_{y \in Y} L_y}$.
For a fixed position $i$ and a fixed set $Y$ of size $N-i$ (where $x \notin Y$), how many permutations have $P_i = x$ and $\{P_{i+1}, \ldots, P_N\} = Y$?
The elements $P_{i+1}, \ldots, P_N$ can be arranged in $(N-i)!$ ways. The elements $P_1, \ldots, P_{i-1}$ can be arranged in $(i-1)!$ ways.
So there are $(N-i)! (i-1)!$ such permutations.
Thus, the total sum is:
$\sum_{i=1}^N \sum_{Y \subseteq \{1, \ldots, N\}, |Y|=N-i, x \notin Y} (N-i)! (i-1)! \
